  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Let $P(x)$ be a polynomial of degree $n\\ge 1$ and let $k\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Show that the number of intersection points of the graphs of $y=P(x)$ and $y=k$ is at most $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "At most $n$ intersections, since each corresponds to a distinct real root of the nonzero degree-$n$ polynomial $P(x)-k$.",
      "worked": "At an intersection, $P(x)=k$, so its $x$-coordinate is a root of $P(x)-k$. Since $n\\ge 1$, subtracting the constant $k$ leaves a nonzero polynomial of degree $n$. By Theorem 1, it has at most $n$ roots counted with multiplicity, and hence at most $n$ distinct real roots. Each distinct real root gives exactly one intersection point $(x,k)$. Therefore, the graphs have at most $n$ intersection points.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
